A||N||4||Cplensdorf||V6 3.2L Rebuild Questions||||1||
Z||000000||Cplensdorf||02-23-2001||09:39 PM||||Just acquired a 'stroked' 2.8.  I was told it was now a 3.2.  <P>How can I determine the displacement?<P>What else needs to be done to complete the rebuild of a stroked 2.8 other than stock components? (i.e. head gaskets, rocker arms, valves, computer mods, throttle body, etc.)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carrolles||02-24-2001||11:18 AM||||The long stroke crank makes a stock 2.8 into a 3.1<P>Boring out the stroked 2.8 by .040 (I believe) makes it a 3.2.<P>Not sure how you could tell what you have without measuring the cylinder bore.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Shaun41178||02-24-2001||06:24 PM||||you won't be able to tell unless you pull the pan off and check the crank for the telltale "3.1" casting on it or pull off the heads and check to see if there is a dish in the piston.  if there is then 99% most likely it is a 3.1||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cplensdorf||02-24-2001||08:38 PM||||It is a short block, so no heads.  The pistons are dished and the crank...well I will check.  What does the stock 2.8 cylinder measure?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Shaun41178||02-25-2001||04:35 PM||||stock bore on a 2.8 and 3.1 is 3.50 inches.  However just because it has been overbored doesn't mean it is a 3.1 liter.  It could still be a 2.8 liter with an overbore.  How deep is the dish in the pistons?  Is it a large dish or just a slight dish to them?  If it seems to be a fairly large dish then it is setup for aluminum heads.  If it is a slight dish then it is setup for iron heads and is a 3.1 liter<P>Take the pan off and look to see if it is a 3.1 crank or not.||||reg||1
